The catalogue, which is the part that matters
---------------------------------------------
xmlconf/xmlconf.xml is a machine-readable catalogue of 2585 test cases.  Each
<TEST> carries the attributes t/suite.py scores against:

  TYPE            valid | invalid | not-wf | error
  ENTITIES        none | general | parameter | both
                  -- whether the case needs an external entity resolved
  VERSION         1.0 | 1.1 | (absent)
  EDITION         "5" | "1 2 3 4" | (absent)
                  -- which editions of XML 1.0 the case applies to
  RECOMMENDATION  XML1.0 | XML1.1 | XML1.0-errata2e/3e/4e | NS1.0 | NS1.1 | ...
  NAMESPACE       yes | no
  URI             the document, relative to the enclosing xml:base

These attributes are the authority's own classification of its own tests.
xmlc's scope filter is expressed in terms of them and nothing else, so the
line between "should have caught" and "out of scope" is drawn by the suite
rather than by us.  The catalogue is itself an XML document with external
parameter entities, so t/suite.py parses it with Python's expat -- which is
also the differential oracle.
